e ptc and the itc  affecting the market for renewable  energy financing and in particular what  you expect whether you have any inkling  as to what to expect  to the itc going forward  um yeah there's i think  two areas of regulatory uncertainty  one is sort of purely a tax code issue  some of you may be familiar with the  historic boardwalk ruling that was  passed down a couple years ago which  sent the historic tax credit market and  those who are active in that space  sideways for a couple of years  because the the nature of the structure  in a particular project in connecticut  um  not connected to yale was challenged um  by the irs  and and that caused um deal structures  and deal economics to really get altered  in the historic tax credit world  things are back on track deals are  getting done tax council is comfortable  with the structures that have been  arrived at  but with any tax credit program you've  got that regulatory risk that suddenly  the the service or treasury department  is going to come in and basically change  the rules change the structures  based on what they're seeing in the  marketplace so that's that's one level  of risk  and that can be dealt with and gets  dealt with as you're structuring your  deal the second risk is the risk that  was referred to in the question directly  which is the risk of program renewal  for these programs that aren't permanent  legislation they become political  footballs and i'm sure you all have  seen these things or heard of these  things coming  up for discussion as  tax policy is debated in washington  i don't have any insight sadly  as to what the future of itc and ptc  credits are that the one thing i will  say is they are in the grand scheme of  things relatively small programs  with a lot of strong advocacy behind  them  so  the little insight that i get is a  feeling that these programs will  continue  into the future indefinitely  likewise with new markets  but i say that with a huge asterisk next  to it because you just never know what's  going to drop off as uh congress is  chopping and cutting various programs so  not a very satisfying answer but that's  all i've got right now  okay thanks for that um the next  question comes from steve  um  and he wanted to know i guess touching  on what jason said in his introduction  uh what the role of green banks and  specifically uh strategies used by green  banks such as pace financing could play  both on the bond side and more generally  in terms of renewable energy financing  yeah um i'll start with that one at  least this is jason so i think that's a  good question  when you talk about the strategies that  green banks can play in the role that  they have in financing so you do see  entities that are  in some cases maybe officially a green  bank although i know that there are a  pretty small number that consider  themselves to be you know using  quotation marks or capital letters green  bank  but some of those entities do have bond  capabilities so of course the main green  bank that people point to is the one in  connecticut  and so the connecticut green bank does  have the ability to issue bonds and they  can do that to support pace they can  support a larger pace portfolio by  taking out kind of a big  chunk of funding to run that pace  program and then  you know bonds bonds have a role there  and setting up that capital  in  other cases an actual green bank or just  one of the development entities that i  talked about that wants to be involved  in energy projects and using bond  financing they can they've got a wide  variety of options so  i believe doug was talking about the  exam facilities bonds or taxes on  private activity bonds and those can be  used for things like airports  and  docks and mass transit facilities  multi-family housing manufacturers so  those are all  water facilities as well water treatment  those are all major  facilities that take a large amount of  capital to finance and to have  constructed and so to the extent that  those facilities are interested in doing  things like making efficiency upgrades  or  installing solar or combining power  biomass whatever it may be  bonds can be issued directly  to those facilities to help  provide lower financing costs on  installing those those clean energy  facilities onto those those locations  those are some good potential users i  know in toledo one of the wastewater  treatment plants up there has taken  advantage of  i believe they did the solar panel  installation  there are other places in the country  that have done it as well  also exempt facility bonds directly can  be used for biomass and for  i think it's  central heat and power combine heat  power as well they can be used directly  for those facilities doesn't matter what  type of other  building or  purpose that they're being put to you  can just issue a private activity bond  for biomass for example  so  i think that answers that basically  bonds can be integral to bonds they  should to a green bank they should be  one of the tools that they're using  because they can provide big chunks of  capital at one time and so you might  have additional thoughts as well of  course  now you know i the only thought i would  add to that jason is that uh  to your point about the breadth of the  bonds  there are a lot of projects that get  done as bond issues without a federal  subsidy involved in other words  they're done for  uh as a  they're done through their traditional  bonding mechanisms of the state without  a federal subsidy attached to it and  that's because there  might be  state and local benefits available  bestowed up by virtue of issuing that  bond and uh lots you know there's  structures  that will be put into place using that  in including sort of lease structures  and other structures that transfer  ownership into a governmental entity to  eliminate taxation  just  that would be the thing i would add on  to it  to what you said jason  yeah that's a great point i appreciate  that and particularly um for people who  probably haven't paid as much attention  as you have  and part of the reason for that you know  you mentioned you can  pair it with other state and local  benefits but then also in the interest  rate environment that we have right now  the benefit of that federal tax  exemption isn't as large as historically  and so i think in  again you can chime in if this is wrong  but i think  that's even more common that people are  doing bonds without that federal tax  exemption because the rates are so close  anyhow  yeah no i think that's generally right  great uh good answers there well um the  next question uh comes from john and  it's directed for doug  and the the question is could you talk  about some of the unique challenges that  exist for either energy efficiency or  renewable energy bonds as distinct from  other more traditional types of bonds  absolutely i think  one of the  one of the unique challenges of  energy efficiency projects  uh always has to do with the timing  of  um  in the matching of the  savings versus the debt service and the  type of facility it's going into  oftentimes you'll see  projects where um  you know the  the payout on the efficiency is shorter  than what they want to amortize the debt  so it can present some challenges there  and working with some of the  energy service companies out there that  are particularly adept at trying to  structure these projects so that they  generate savings that you know  essentially paid at that service and  also meet a variety of state rules or  requirements about how those savings  uh have to uh  be structured in order that a bond issue  can or  any sort of obligation could be issued  on a state or local level for that does  you know i would see the matching of  that uh savings and revenue  or debt service rather it's a critical  piece there on the energy efficiency  side  clearly as pace develops on energy you  know  across a variety of different platforms  whether it's renewable or energy  efficiency it'll be interesting to see  how that works on the commercial side as  well um  as it um  as local governmental entities set up  programs to allow for uh you know  assessments or what have you to be  uh pledged um  uh to  uh for commercial projects and and the  matching of those revenues and and  whether commercial entities will want to  have  as long as um amortization varies as  maybe a governmental entity whether  their budget concerns are different so i  could see those as being future  challenges there  on the renewable side i would  i would really point to the fact a  common column made about scalability  one of the one of the issues you see on  a lot of these projects is  the size of it and  the  funding the sources of funding for it  there's not a lot of familiarity in a  lot of places with  solar or wind at the governmental level  just in terms of  the finance side you know the engineers  may be very experienced with it  and so oftentimes the challenges are  just uh  those kind of two different mindsets  meeting and trying to get together on a  project i think in the last five to ten  years you've seen a monumental  evolution on  and  and  in terms of understanding how these  projects work and what they can do  um  you know one of the key areas we're  seeing a lot on the utility side is just  the kind of biogas  world so whether it's water wastewater  or even  kind of solid waste utilities trying to  figure out how to  generate a revenue stream  or  save uh  uh you know  lower their expense profile by capturing  um some of these uh resources and uh  reusing them  and you know i would say that you know  the big challenge there in a lot of  those is just the size of the project  and whether or not you can put something  together and have the time and attention  bringing the third party  professionals in i think that's a  real reason why when folks put these  projects together it's really important  to try to  bring in folks that have done them  before to try to address those issues  so it you know  those challenges can be overcome that's  particularly scalability but those would  be the two  great thanks for that uh so we are  running out of time but i think we have  time for two more questions um  first one comes from  stewart for colin um there was a mention  of a reasonable scale of projects  and stu was wondering what in terms of  dollar  values  is this reasonable scale when it comes  to tax credit and bond financing  i can speak on the tax credit side i  might defer to one of the other  panelists on the bond side for for new  markets  typically you're looking at seven  million dollars if total project costs  were larger  anything below that you really start to  dilute the benefit because of some of  the fixed costs that are incurred in  getting the deals closed the one caveat  i would throw in if you have a highly  impactful  project that's smaller than that less  than five is really tough but five to  seven million dollars where you can  really draw a bright line to the  benefits to a low-income person that  that can get done uh and i would say on  the energy side  it's it's probably similar um  larger than 10 is preferred um it's hard  to get the tax equity first deals that  are smaller than 10 million in total  project cost  it's possible if all the other pieces  are in place a big strong guarantee and  a good credit worthy developer  but those are the sort of general  parameters i would offer on the bond  side i don't know doug if you have  insight you can share yeah and  and that is um  goes to really the ownership question i  mean i've seen a lot of very small  purely governmental projects get done on  the bond side because it's easier to  potentially uh just to include them in  a variety of other mechanisms um it's  the scalability issue really becomes  when it's um there's some sort of public  private um that maybe there's private  ownership involved and it really has to  be a bigger project and i would  i don't know that um  i can speak  uh  to what this you know the specific  number would be  uh but  when it's a  corporate any or non-governmental entity  doing a bond for  a project you know i would say typically  they don't want to  really dip below seven and a half  million dollars unless there are other  things going on beside that bond that  allow it to  allow that the time and expense of  putting it together  to make sense  so you know i i hates draw  bright lines but i think anytime you're  looking below seven and a half million  you should start figuring out how you're  gonna pay for it and and uh  over a period of time and whether it  really makes sense from that uh from  that standpoint  and i you know largely would be  deferential to any sort of financial  advisor on that that's more of a  business sort of question than a legal  question  great thanks for that okay so we are out  of time so this will be the last  question um and it's posed by david uh  for jason i think  is there a good resource that describes  the different finance authorities  throughout the country  yeah that's i'm trying to think if  there's a good one  online certainly if you go to  our website cdfa.net we have an online  resource database that includes  resources on a wide variety of finance  tools  you'll be able to find information on  all the different agencies that i  mentioned and quite a few others on our  site  i think probably the best  resource i can think of that's going to  describe the different agencies and the  types of programs different agencies  offer is a book that we have called the  drone finance practitioners guide  but  i'm sure we have an online one if you  want to follow me with email i'm sure i  can send you something  the type of nc's i'm talking about state  level agencies and local level agencies  um  and so if you just kind of look around  in your community they tend not to be  too hard 